---
title: "Final Summary"
subtitle: "What the simulations say about sample size, shape, and assumptions"
format: html
---

```{r}
source("R/helpers.R")
student_id <- 6599L  # Replace with the last four digits of your student ID.
B <- B_DEFAULT
studies <- run_all_studies(seed = student_id, B = B, keep_samples = FALSE)
summary <- summary_table(studies)
```

## Research question

The project asks whether there is a universal sample size at which a sampling
distribution of a mean can be called approximately normal. I tested that idea
with the same Monte Carlo design across populations with different shapes and
assumption structures.

## Experimental design

For each population and each candidate \(n\), I generated `r paste0("B = ", B)` sample
means. The candidate sizes were `r paste(N_VALUES, collapse = ", ")`. The
student seed is set immediately before every experiment. This makes each row
reproducible and allows a second seed to be used as a sensitivity check.

The operational definition of “approximately normal” requires all four of the
following:

- absolute skewness ≤ 0.20;
- absolute excess kurtosis ≤ 0.30;
- normal Q-Q correlation ≥ 0.995;
- standardized normal Q-Q RMSE ≤ 0.08.

The selected \(n\) is the first candidate that passes all four criteria for
three consecutive investigated sizes. This stability requirement prevents a
single Monte Carlo fluctuation from determining the answer. Histograms,
theoretical standard deviations, and the population assumptions are reviewed
alongside the numerical screen.

## Cross-distribution comparison

```{r}
display_summary <- summary
display_summary$smallest_n_judged_normal <- ifelse(
  is.na(display_summary$smallest_n_judged_normal),
  "none through n = 500",
  as.character(display_summary$smallest_n_judged_normal)
)
knitr::kable(display_summary, digits = 3)
```

The table is generated directly from the same helper functions used in the
individual analyses. The exact values depend on the student seed, but the
relative pattern is the main scientific result.

## Answer to the research question

The answer is ambiguous because the question asks for a smallest value of
\(n\), but the phrase “reasonably defensible as approximately normal” depends
on several choices. It is not a property that switches on at one universal
sample size.

First, the answer depends on the population being averaged. In this project,
the current run uses seed `r student_id` and `r paste0("B = ", B)` repetitions. The
first stable passes were:

| Population type | First stable pass |
|---|---:|
| Standard normal | \(n=2\) |
| Continuous uniform | \(n=5\) |
| Symmetric binomial, size 20 | \(n=5\) |
| Six-sided die | \(n=10\) |
| Poisson(1) | \(n=30\) |
| Binomial(5, .10) | \(n=50\) |
| Two-component mixture | \(n=50\) |
| Non-identical Bernoulli | \(n=75\) |
| Exponential(1) | \(n=100\) |
| Dependent process | No stable pass through \(n=500\) |
| Cauchy | No stable pass through \(n=500\) |

These results are evidence against treating \(n=30\) as a universal rule. For
example, the Poisson population did pass at \(n=30\), with skewness about
0.142, excess kurtosis about -0.007, Q-Q correlation about 0.998, and Q-Q RMSE
about 0.064. By contrast, the Exponential population still had skewness about
0.398 at \(n=30\), so it failed the same standard; it first passed at \(n=100\),
where skewness was about 0.189. The rare-success binomial population passed at
\(n=50\), not at the conventional rule-of-thumb value of 30.

Second, the answer depends on what “approximately normal” means. I made the
judgment reproducible by requiring all four shape criteria—absolute skewness at
most 0.20, absolute excess kurtosis at most 0.30, Q-Q correlation at least
0.995, and standardized Q-Q RMSE at most 0.08. I also required three
consecutive passing candidate sizes. A researcher using a looser skewness
threshold, a different Q-Q tolerance, or only visual inspection could select a
smaller value. That does not necessarily mean one analysis is wrong; it means
the analyses answer slightly different operational questions.

Third, “smallest” is relative to the sample sizes investigated. The simulation
tested \(n=2,5,10,15,20,30,40,50,75,100,150,200,300,500\). Thus, for example,
the reported \(n=100\) for the exponential population means the first stable
passing value on this grid. It does not prove that every value between 75 and
100 fails. A finer search could refine the boundary.

Fourth, Monte Carlo results are estimates of the sampling distributions, not
perfect observations of them. Changing the random seed changes the exact
skewness, kurtosis, and Q-Q measurements. That is why the project records the
seed, uses 10,000 repetitions, and requires consecutive passes. The broad
conclusions are more meaningful than the last decimal place.

Finally, the usual CLT assumptions matter. The Cauchy sample means did not
produce a stable normal approximation through \(n=500\); at \(n=500\), the
simulated skewness was about 87.46, excess kurtosis about 8213.51, and Q-Q
correlation only about 0.093. The dependent process also failed at \(n=500\),
with skewness about 0.282 even though its Q-Q correlation was about 0.998. A
large nominal sample size does not automatically create a large amount of
independent information.

Therefore, the most professional answer is: **there is no single universal
smallest sample size.** For a specified population, diagnostic standard,
simulation design, and purpose, the smallest defensible value is the first
sample size whose evidence meets the stated standard. In this project, that
value ranges from \(n=2\) to \(n=100\) for the finite-variance independent
populations, while the dependent and Cauchy cases do not meet the standard
within the investigated range.

## Population shape

Symmetry is associated with faster convergence in these experiments. The
exponential, rare-success binomial, and two-component mixture cases retain
right-skew or tail behavior longer. The Poisson case is also skewed, but its
skewness is less extreme than the rare-success binomial case. These differences
show why the population distribution matters when choosing a practical sample
size.

## Assumptions and limitations

The Cauchy experiment is the exception to the finite-variance pattern: no stable
sample-mean normality was found through \(n=500\), which is consistent with its
lack of a finite mean and variance. The dependent process shows why nominal
sample size can overstate the amount of independent information. The
non-identical Bernoulli process shows that changing probabilities affect the
variance calculation even when a bell-shaped average still develops.

The dependent machine-failure and mixture analyses use explicit models because
the handout labels those population specifications as “coming soon.” If the
instructor supplies official generators, those generator functions should be
replaced while keeping the documented design and diagnostic rule unchanged.

## Reproducibility and seed sensitivity

```{r}
selected <- summary$smallest_n_judged_normal
selected <- selected[!is.na(selected)]
if (length(selected) > 0) {
  first_population <- names(studies)[which(!is.na(summary$smallest_n_judged_normal))[1]]
  n_for_check <- studies[[first_population]]$selected_n
  reproducibility_check(first_population, n = n_for_check,
                        seed_a = student_id, seed_b = 2026L, B = B)
}
```

The exact simulated means, skewness, and kurtosis change when the seed changes.
The conclusion is considered reproducible when the broad decision—such as
“passes early,” “requires a large \(n\),” or “no stable \(n\) in the range”—does
not change. The Cauchy case is expected to be the most seed-sensitive because
its tails do not provide stable ordinary moments.

## Overall conclusion

The Central Limit Theorem describes an asymptotic tendency, not a universal
sample-size switch. A responsible normal approximation requires attention to
population shape, dependence, identical-distribution assumptions, tail behavior,
and the purpose of the approximation. Monte Carlo simulation makes those
differences visible and provides evidence for a population-specific judgment.

